&= m+m \sum_{i=2}^{m} \int_{i-1}^{i} \frac{dx}{i} 
& \leq m+m \sum_{i=2}^{m} \int_{i-1}^{i} \frac{dx}{x} \ \ \dots \text{since } \frac{1}{x} \ \text{is strictly decreasing} 
&= m+m \int_{1}^{m} \frac{dx}{x} 
&= m (1+\ln m) 
&< 10^{100} m \ln (m+1)
\end{align*}</details>)

Now, fix a large positive integer  $M$ . We will count the number of pairs  $(m,n)$  positive integers such that  $m \leq M$  and  $f(m) \geq n^2$ . Firstly, for a fixed  $m$ , there are at least  $\lfloor \sqrt{m} \rfloor \geq \frac{\sqrt{m}}{1000}$  values of  $n$  satisfying  $f(m) \geq n^2$ . Therefore there are at least   $$ \frac{1}{1000} \sum_{k=1}^{M} \sqrt{k} \geq \frac{M^{\frac32}}{10^{100}} $$  such pairs. (<details><summary>Details for bound</summary>\begin{align*}
