By Pascal,  $A,B,C,D',E',F'$  lying on a conic is equivalent to  $X=AD' \cap BF'$ ,  $Y=BE' \cap CD'$ , and  $Z=CF' \cap AE'$  being collinear. Note that  $X,Y,Z$  are on  $PD$ ,  $PE$ , and  $PF$ ,

Now, suppose  $X$ ,  $Y$ ,  $Z$  are collinear. We have that for some fixed constants  $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$ , by the hidden claim,
\[DBC\text{ collinear } \implies \frac{\alpha}{PD}+\frac{\beta}{PB}+\frac{\gamma}{PC}=0\]
\[AEC\text{ collinear } \implies \frac{\alpha}{PA}+\frac{\beta}{PE}+\frac{\gamma}{PC}=0\]
\[ABF\text{ collinear } \implies \frac{\alpha}{PA}+\frac{\beta}{PB}+\frac{\gamma}{PF}=0\]
\[XBF'\text{ collinear} \implies \frac{\alpha}{PX}+\frac{\beta}{PB}+\frac{\gamma}{\lambda PF}=0\]
\[D'YC\text{ collinear} \implies \frac{\alpha}{\lambda PD}+\frac{\beta}{PY}+\frac{\gamma}{PC}=0\]
\[AE'Z\text{ collinear} \implies \frac{\alpha}{PA}+\frac{\beta}{\lambda PE}+\frac{\gamma}{PZ}=0\]
\[XYZ\text{ collinear} \implies \frac{\alpha}{PX}+\frac{\beta}{PY}+\frac{\gamma}{PZ}=0\]
Summing these up with coefficients  $1,1,1,-2,-2,-2,2$  gives
\[\left(1-\frac{2}{\lambda}\right) \left(\frac{\alpha}{PD}+\frac{\beta}{PE}+\frac{\gamma}{PF}\right)=0.\]
Since  $DEF$  are not collinear, the second term is nonzero, so we must have  $\lambda=2$ , as desired.

We claim that the answer to part (a) is yes. Note that we have  $a_{n+1}\equiv 2a_n\pmod p$  and  $\Delta a_n=d_p(a_1^n)$ . Take any prime  $p\equiv 1\pmod 4$ , and set
\begin{align*}
a_0&=\frac{3p+1}{4}
b_0&=p+1
\end{align*}
Then  $a_0<b_0$  but \[a_1=\frac{3p+1}{2}>p+2=b_1\] We also have that  $a_0$  and  $b_0$  belong to the same cycle of the map  $x\mapsto 2x$  in  $(\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z)^\times$ . In particular, letting  $k=\operatorname{ord}_p(2)$ , we have that  $(\Delta a_0,\dots,\Delta a_{k-1})$  and  $(\Delta b_0,\dots,\Delta b_{k-1})$  are permutations. It follows that \[a_{n+k}-a_n=b_{n+k}-b_n\] for all  $n$ . Then \begin{align*} a_n < b_n & \text{ for }n\equiv 0\pmod k a_n>b_n & \text{ for }n\equiv 1\pmod k\end{align*} which satisfies the needed condition.

We claim that the answer to part (b) is yes. Take any odd prime  $p$  for which  $k=\operatorname{ord}_p(2)$  is odd; we can do this by taking prime divisors of  $2^t-1$  for  $t$  odd, of which there are infinitely many. Then the map  $x\mapsto 2x$  in  $(\mathbb Z/p\mathbb Z)^\times$  can be decomposed into  $\frac{p-1}{k}$  cycles of length  $k$ . Considering the remainders corresponding to the residues in these cycles, the average value of the average value of the remainders in each cycle over all cycles is  $\frac{p}{2}$ . Then some cycle has average value  $m\geq \frac{p}{2}$ . Since the length  $k$  of the cycle is odd, we have  $m>\frac{p}{2}$ . Let  $r$  be an element of this cycle. Now take
\begin{align*}
a_0&=r
b_0&=2p-r
\end{align*}
Then  $a_0<b_0$ . Also note that the elements of the cycle to which  $p-r$  belongs are just the elements of the form  $p-x$  where  $x$  is the corresponding element of the cycle to which  $r$  belongs. Then the average values of  $(\Delta a_0,\dots,\Delta a_{k-1})$  and  $(\Delta b_0,\dots,\Delta b_{k-1})$  are  $m$  and  $p-m$  respectively. So, we have
\begin{align*}
a_n&\sim mn
b_n&\sim (p-m)n
\end{align*}
In particular,  $a_n>b_n$  for all sufficiently large  $n$ . Then we can truncate the sequences  $\{a_n\},\{b_n\}$  up to the last term with  $a_n<b_n$  to get the desired  $p$ -sequences.

