<span style="color:red">**Claim:**</span>  $B$  is the circumcenter of  $\triangle AEX$ . Similarly,  $D$  is the circumcenter of  $\triangle AFX$ .

*Proof:* Let  $\angle ADB = \theta$ ,  $\angle BDX = \beta$ ,  $\angle BAK = \angle BXK = \alpha$ ,  $\angle BAD = \angle BXD = \phi$ .

Applying Law of Sines in  $\triangle BAK$  and  $\triangle BKX$  we get
        \begin{align}
            \frac{KA}{\sin(\phi + \theta)}= \frac{KB}{\sin(\alpha)} = \frac{KX}{\sin(\phi + \beta)} \label{eq1}
        \end{align}
 Again apply the Law of Sines in  $\triangle KAD$  and  $\triangle KAX$  to get
        \begin{align*}
            \frac{KA}{\sin(\theta)}= \frac{KD}{\sin(\theta + \alpha)} = \frac{KX}{\sin(\beta)} \tag{2}
        \end{align*}
Combining  $(1)$  and  $(2)$  to get
        \begin{align*}
            \frac{\sin(\phi + \theta)}{\sin(\phi + \beta)} = \frac{\sin(\theta)}{\sin(\beta)}
        \end{align*}
Expanding to get
        \begin{align*}
            \frac{\sin(\phi)\cos(\theta)+ \sin(\theta)\cos(\phi)}{\sin(\phi)\cos(\beta)+\sin(\beta)\cos(\phi)}= \frac{\sin(\theta)}{\sin(\beta)}
        \end{align*}
        On cross multiplying we get
        \begin{align*}
            \sin(\phi)\sin(\beta)\cos(\theta) + \cancel{\sin(\theta)\sin(\beta)\cos(\phi)} & = \sin(\phi)\sin(\theta)\cos(\beta) + \cancel{\sin(\theta)\sin(\beta)\cos(\phi)} 
            \sin(\beta)\cos(\theta)                                                        & = \sin(\theta)\cos(\beta)                                                        
            \tan(\theta)                                                                   & = \tan(\beta)                                                                    
            \implies \theta                                                                & = \beta
        \end{align*}
Rest follows immediately from $\triangle BAD \cong \triangle BXD$ .  $\square$ It is easy to show that  $X \in \odot(DFL)$  as  $\measuredangle DFA = \measuredangle FAD = \measuredangle DXL$ . Proceeding note that  $AYCZ$  is cyclic with diameter  $YZ$ . Therefore  $\measuredangle YCZ = \measuredangle YAZ$ . To finish the problem, it suffices to show that  $\measuredangle BXD = \measuredangle BKX + \measuredangle XLD$ .
    \begin{align*}
        \measuredangle BXD = \measuredangle BCD & = \measuredangle YCZ                      
                                                & = \measuredangle YAZ                      
                                                & = \measuredangle KAL                      
                                                & = \measuredangle AKL + \measuredangle KLA 
                                                & = \measuredangle BKX + \measuredangle XLD
    \end{align*}
which completes the solution.  $\blacksquare$

Reflect  $A$  about  $BD$  to a point  $A'$ , and note that  $A'BDC$  is cyclic.**Claim:**  $(BEK)$  passes through  $A'$ .
*Proof.* Immediately follows from   $$ \angle BA'K = \angle BAK = \angle BEA $$   proving the claim.  $\square$ Likewise  $(DFL)$  passes through  $A'$ . Now let  $T$  on  $(A'BDC)$  be chosen so that  $CT \perp BD$ . **Claim:**  $A'T$  is the common internal tangent to  $(BEK)$  and  $(DFL)$ .
*Proof.* Proceed with complex numbers, setting  $(A'BDC)$  as the unit circle. Then we compute,


- $t = -bd/c$
- $a = b + d - bd/a'$
- $e = b + c - bc\overline{a} = b - bc/d + a'c/d$

Then to verify  $\angle TA'B = \angle A'EB$  we have to check,
\begin{align*}
\frac{t - a'}{b - a'} \div \frac{a' - e}{b - e} \in \mathbb{R}
\end{align*}
However this follows as,
\begin{align*}
\frac{t - a'}{b - a'} \div \frac{a' - e}{b - e} &= \frac{-bd/c - a'}{b - a'} \div \frac{a' - b + bc/d - a'c/d}{bc/d - a'c/d}
&= \frac{bd + a'c}{a'c - bc} \div \frac{a'd - bd + bc - a'c}{bc - a'c}
&= \frac{bd + a'c}{a'c - bc} \div \frac{(b - a')(c - d)}{c(b - a')}
&= \frac{bd + a'c}{(a' - b)(c - d)} 
\end{align*}
which is clearly self conjugate. Thus both circles are tangent at  $A'$  and we're done.  $\square$

We show that there are exactly  $\boxed{F_{n + 1}}$  permutations, where  $F$  denotes the fibonacci numbers.**Claim:**
In the permutation,  $i \in \{ a_{i - 1}, a_i, a_{i + 1} \}$  for all  $i$ *Proof.*
We'll use strong induction. The base case of  $1$  is apparent, as if  $1$  was at position  $j > 2$ , 
we would have  $a_{j - 1}(j - 1) > 2(j - 1) > 1(j)$ . 

Suppose FTSOC that  $i$  appears at position  $j < i - 1$ . 
Then  $j$  cannot appear at position  $j + 1$  because  $ij > j(j + 1)$ . Thus by induction,   $j$  must appear at   $a_{j - 1}$ . Now,  $j - 1$  must appear at  $a_{j - 2}$  by induction,  and continuing downward, we obtain  $a_1 = 2$ . But then  $1$  has nowhere to be, so this case cannot happen.

Now if  $i$  appears at position  $j > i + 1$ . Because  $(i + 1)(j - 1)  > ij$ , we must have  $a_{j - 1} < i$ . But then the number that appears at  $a_{j - 1}$  also violates our claim and is smaller than  $i$ , which violates the induction hypothesis.

Now there is a bijection between such permutations and tilings of a $n \times 1$  grid with squares and dominoes. 
Place the numbers from  $1$  to  $n$  in such a grid, and put the tiling upon it. If two numbers are both part of the same domino, then switch the order of the numbers. We can see that all permutations satisfying the claim can be generated in this way,  so this scheme is indeed a bijection. It's well known that the number of such tilings is  $F_{n + 1}$ , so we're done.

