so  $\triangle OCF$  is isosceles with  $OC = OF$ . In particular,  $O$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{CF}$ , so  $O$  is the midpoint of a chord of  $(ACB)$  parallel to  $CF$ . However,  $O$  is also the midpoint of chord  $\overline{AB}$ , which is not parallel to  $CF$ , so  $O$  is the center of  $(ACB)$ . Thus,  $\overline{AB}$  is a diameter of  $(ACB)$ , implying the desired result.  $\blacksquare$

Add in the midpoint  $M$  of  $PQ$  and the midpoint  $N$  of  $AB$ . The key claim is the following which is independent of  $P, E, F$  collinear: **<span style="color:#f00">Claim: </span>** Points  $F, E, Q, M$  are concyclic, as are points  $F, E, N, A$ .
Observe that since  $N$  is the arc midpoint of minor arc  $QP$  as  $\measuredangle ACN = \measuredangle NCB$ , we can recharacterize  $E$  as the intersection of  $\overline{MN}$  and  $\overline{AC}$ . Additionally, since  $F$  is the spiral center of  $\overline{QMP} \implies \overline{ANB}$ , we have \begin{align*}
    \measuredangle FQE = \measuredangle FQA = \measuredangle FMN = \measuredangle FME,
\end{align*} as well as \begin{align*}
    \measuredangle FAE = \measuredangle FAQ = \measuredangle FNM = \measuredangle FNE,
\end{align*} proving the concyclicities.  $\square$ Since  $FEMQ$  is cyclic, we have  $\measuredangle EMQ = \measuredangle EFQ = 90^\circ$ . At last we add in the  $P, E, F$  collinear: since  $CFQDNP$  is cyclic, and since  $\measuredangle EFQ = \measuredangle PFQ = 90^\circ$ , we have  $90^\circ = \measuredangle PFQ = \measuredangle PCQ = \measuredangle BCA$  as desired.

