Denote  $C'$  to be the antipode of  $C$  with respect to  $(ABC)$ . Note that  $\measuredangle CFD = \measuredangle CFC' = 90$ , so  $F,D,C'$  are collinear. We wish to show that  $\measuredangle FPQ = \measuredangle EPQ$  is only possible if  $\measuredangle ACB$  is a right angle (all angles are directed). We now do some angle-chasing based simplification.**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  	 $F,E,B$  are collinear iff  $\measuredangle FBA + \measuredangle DCB = 90$ .
*Proof.*  	We chase: 	\[ 		\measuredangle FPQ = \measuredangle FCQ = \measuredangle FCA = \measuredangle FBA 	\]  	(alternatively cite spiral similarity.) Furthermore, 	\[ 	\measuredangle EPQ = \measuredangle PQF = \measuredangle PQC = \measuredangle PDC = 90 - \measuredangle DCB 	\] 	which finishes our claim.  $\blacksquare$ Now, the points  $E,P,Q$  are completely irrelevant. The rest is some more basic angle chasing: \[ 	\measuredangle FBA + \measuredangle DCB = \measuredangle C'DB - \measuredangle C'FB + \measuredangle C'CB + \measuredangle DCC' = \measuredangle C'DB + \measuredangle DCC' \] so we must have  $90 - \measuredangle C'DB = \measuredangle DCC'$ . However,  $90 - \measuredangle C'DB = \measuredangle CC'D$ , and therefore  $C,D,C'$  must be an isosceles triangle, clearly only possible when  $C$  and  $C'$  are reflections over  $AB$ , i.e.,  $AB$  includes the diameter (and therefore  $ACB$  is a right triangle).

Note the spiral similarity from  $F$  mapping  $\overline{PQ} \mapsto \overline{BA}$ . Then from some simple angle chasing we may conclude,
\begin{align*}
\angle FCA &= \angle FBA
 &= \angle FPQ
&= \angle EQP
\end{align*}
Then  $\overline{PQ} \parallel \overline{CF}$  and  $FCPQ$  is an isosceles trapezoid. Now let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{AB}$  which lies on  $(FCPQD)$  as  $ABC$  is isosceles. Now we claim  $MP = MQ$  which is visible after some simple trig outlined below,
\begin{align*}
MQ &= \frac{AM \cdot \sin A }{\sin(\angle AQM)}
&= \frac{BC \cdot \sin B}{\sin(\angle AQD + \angle DQM)}
&= \frac{BC \cdot \sin B}{\sin(\angle BPD + \angle DPM)}
&= \frac{BC \cdot \sin B}{\sin(\angle MPB)}
&= MP
\end{align*}
Thus  $\overline{EM}$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{PQ}$ . However the  $M$  is the intersection of two of the perpendicular bisectors of the cyclic quad  $ABCF$  namely of  $\overline{FC}$  and  $\overline{AB}$  so  $M$  is the center and thus  $\angle ACB = 90$ .  $\square$

Since  $\angle FPQ = \angle CQP$  and  $F$  lies on  $(CPQ)$ , it follows that  $\overline{CF} \parallel \overline{PQ}$ . Then, since  $\angle CFD = 90^{\circ}$ , it follows that line  $FD$  passes through the antipode of  $C$  WRT  $(ABC)$  – call this point  $M$ . We also have from  $\angle CFM = 90^{\circ}$  that line  $DM$  is perpendicular to line  $PQ$ . From this, we now have
\begin{align*}\angle BDM &= \angle PDM - \angle PDB = \angle DPQ + \angle CBA - 180^{\circ} 
&= \left( 180^{\circ} - 2 \angle CBA - \angle BCD \right) + \angle CBA - 180^{\circ} = \angle CDB,
\end{align*}
implying by symmetry (and the fact that  $D$  is not the midpoint of  $\overline{AB}$ ) that  $\angle BCA = 90^{\circ}$ .

