<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>We already have  $H+V=3333$ . Color the board alternately as @IndoMathXdZ's solution, and note that all  $3\times 1$  tile will cover one red, one green, and one blue tiles. Also, all  $1\times 3$  tile will cover either three red, three green, or three blue tiles. Suppose  $H_R$ ,  $H_G$ ,  $H_B$  are the number of tiles that cover three red, green, and blue squares respectively.

Counting the number of covered red/green/blue tiles, since there are at most one uncovered tile, we have \begin{align*}
&3399\leq V+3H_R \leq 3400,
&3299\leq V+3H_G \leq 3300,
&3299\leq V+3H_B \leq 3300.
\end{align*}

In particular, we have \begin{align*}
V \leq 3300 \implies H\geq 33.
\end{align*}
On another hand, these three expression must be the same modulo 3, hence all of them should be  $0\pmod{3}$ . Hence,  \begin{align*}
V+3H_R=3399 \implies V\equiv 0\pmod{3}.
\end{align*}

Repeating the same argument, we also have  $H\equiv 0\pmod{3}$ , and  $33\leq V,H \leq 3300$ .</details>

