Here's another complex bash (first one was post #13) using a different unit circle and a different characterization of the tangency point, and much less synthetic work.

<details><summary>Setup and computing the three lines that bound I_AEF</summary>Drawing multiple diagrams, see that the tangency point appears to lie on  $(BICI_A)$ . Thus, we set  $(BICI_A)$  to be the unit circle and bash, writing everything in terms of  $b$ ,  $c$ , and  $j$ .

We quickly derive  $a=\frac{j^2+bc}{b+c}$  and  $d=\frac{j^2+bj+cj-bc}{2j}$ .

Line  $BI_A$  has equation  $\ell_1(z)=-z+bj\overline z+b-j=0$  and line  $CI_A$  has equation  $\ell_2(z)=-z+cj\overline z+c-j=0$ .

To find the equation of line  $AD$ , we set
\[\left|\begin{matrix}z&\overline z&1a&\overline a&1d&\overline d&1\end{matrix}\right|=0\]

We now compute the coefficients of  $z$ ,  $\overline z$ , and  $1$ .
\begin{align*}
\overline a-\overline d&=\frac{j^2+bc}{j^2(b+c)}-\frac{-j^2+bj+cj+bc}{2jbc}
&=\frac{2bc(j^2+bc)-j(b+c)(-j^2+bj+cj+bc)}{2j^2bc(b+c)}
&=\frac{(b+c)j^3-(b^2+c^2)j^2-bc(b+c)j+2b^2c^2}{2j^2bc(b+c)}
&=\frac{(b-j)(c-j)(bj+cj+2bc)}{2j^2bc(b+c)}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
d-a&=-\overline{\frac{(b-j)(c-j)(bj+cj+2bc)}{2j^2bc(b+c)}}
&=-\frac{(b-j)(c-j)(b+c+2j)}{2j(b+c)}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
a\overline d-\overline ad&=\frac{j^2+bc}{b+c}\cdot\frac{-j^2+bj+cj+bc}{2jbc}-\frac{j^2+bc}{j^2(b+c)}\cdot\frac{j^2+bj+cj-bc}{2j}
&=\frac{(j^2+bc)(j^2(-j^2+bj+cj+bc)-bc(j^2+bj+cj-bc))}{2j^3bc(b+c)}
&=\frac{(j^2+bc)(-j^4+(b+c)j^3-bc(b+c)j+b^2c^2)}{2j^3bc(b+c)}
&=\frac{(b-j)(c-j)(j^2+bc)(-j^2+bc)}{2j^3bc(b+c)}
\end{align*}

Note that we knew  $(b-j)(c-j)$  would be a factor of each numerator because  $b=j$  or  $c=j$  implies  $a=d$ .

Cancelling common factors and clearing denominators, we have that the equation of line  $AD$  is
\[\ell_3(z)=j(bj+cj+2bc)z-bcj^2(b+c+2j)\overline z+(j^2+bc)(-j^2+bc)=0\]</details>
<details><summary>Computing (I_AEF)</summary>Now, the equation of circle  $(I_AEF)$  is
\[C(z)=k_1\ell_2(z)\ell_3(z)+k_2\ell_3(z)\ell_1(z)+k_3\ell_1(z)\ell_2(z)=0\]
where  $k_1$ ,  $k_2$ , and  $k_3$  are chosen such that the  $z^2$  and  $\overline z^2$  terms vanish.

Since the  $z^2$  coefficient vanishes, we have  $k_3=j(bj+cj+2bc)(k_1+k_2)$ .

Since the  $\overline z^2$  coefficient vanishes, we have  $k_3=j(b+c+2j)(ck_1+bk_2)$ .

Subtracting the two equations gives  $j((bj-cj+bc-c^2)k_1+(-bj+cj+bc-b^2)k_2)=0$ , or  $j(b-c)((j+c)k_1-(j+b)k_2)=0$ .

Thus, we set  $k_1=j+b$  and  $k_2=j+c$ , giving  $k_3=j(b+c+2j)(bj+cj+2bc)$ .</details>
<details><summary>Computing the other intersection of (I_AEF) and (BICI_A)</summary>We know that one of the intersections of  $(I_AEF)$  with the unit circle is  $I_A$ , or  $-j$ , and we want to find the other intersection,  $t$ . By Vieta's, the product of the complex coordinates of the two intersections is the ratio of the  $\overline z$  and  $z$  coefficients of  $C(z)$ , or
\begin{align*}
&\frac{(j+b)(cj(j^2+bc)(-j^2+bc)-bcj^2(b+c+2j)(c-j))+(j+c)(bj(j^2+bc)(-j^2+bc)-bcj^2(b+c+2j)(b-j))+j(b+c+2j)(bj+cj+2bc)(bj(c-j)+cj(b-j))}{(j+b)(-(j^2+bc)(-j^2+bc)+j(bj+cj+2bc)(c-j))+(j+c)(-(j^2+bc)(-j^2+bc)+j(bj+cj+2bc)(b-j))+j(b+c+2j)(bj+cj+2bc)(b-j+c-j)}
&=\frac{j(-(b+c)j^5-2(b^2+bc+c^2)j^4-(b+c)(b^2+c^2)j^3+4b^2c^2j^2+3b^2c^2(b+c)j+2b^3c^3)}{2j^5+3(b+c)j^4bcj^3-(b+c)(b^2+c^2)j^2-2bc(b^2+bc+c^2)j-b^2c^2(b+c)}
&=\frac{j(b+j)^2(c+j)^2(2bc-bj-cj)}{(b+j)^2(c+j)^2(2j-b-c)}
&=\frac{j(2bc-bj-cj)}{2j-b-c}
\end{align*}
So the other intersection is  $t=\frac{2bc-bj-cj}{b+c-2j}$ .</details>
<details><summary>Showing it lies on (AID)</summary>We want
\begin{align*}
\frac{(t-a)(j-d)}{(t-j)(a-d)}&\stackrel?\in\mathbb R
\frac{(\frac{2bc-bj-cj}{b+c-2j}-\frac{j^2+bc}{b+c})(b-c)}{(\frac{2bc-bj-cj}{b+c-2j}-j)(\frac{(b-j)(c-j)(b+c+2j)}{2j(b+c)})}&\stackrel?\in i\mathbb R
\frac{2j((b+c)(2bc-bj-cj)-(b+c-2j)(j^2+bc))(b-c)}{(2bc-bj-cj-j(b+c-2j))(b-j)(c-j)(b+c+2j)}&\stackrel?\in i\mathbb R
\frac{j(b-j)(c-j)(b+c+2j)(b-c)}{(b-j)^2(c-j)^2(b+c+2j)}&\stackrel?\in i\mathbb R
\frac{j(b-c)}{(b-j)(c-j)}&\stackrel?\in i\mathbb R
\end{align*}
which is true by inspection.</details>
<details><summary>Showing the circles are tangent</summary>The equation of the tangent to  $(AIDT)$  at  $T$  is  $\frac{(z-t)(a-j)}{(t-a)(t-j)}\in\mathbb R$ .
