Split  $[1,2^k]$  into intervals  $[2^a,2^{a+1}]$  for  $a\in\{0,1,\dots,k-1\}$ . Then the sum for the  $m$  terms in this interval is
\begin{align*}
&\le\frac{2^{a+1}}{\sqrt{2^{2a}}}+\frac{2^{a+1}}{\sqrt{2\cdot2^{2a}}}+\dots+\frac{2^{a+1}}{\sqrt{m\cdot 2^{2a}}} 
&=2\left(1+2+\dots+\frac{1}{\sqrt m}\right) 
&<2\left(\int_0^m\frac{\mathrm dx}{\sqrt x}\right) 
&=4\sqrt{m}.
\end{align*}
Now by Jensen,
\[\sum_a 4\sqrt{m}\le 4\sqrt{\frac{n}{k}}\cdot k=4\sqrt{nk},\]
as desired.  $\blacksquare$

