Let  $PX,QX$  meet  $(ABC)$  again in  $J,K$ . $Claim.$   $MN$  is tangent to the incircle of  $ABC$   $Proof.$  Observe that 
\begin{align*}
\angle MIN&=\angle MBI+\angle NCI
&=\frac{B}{2}+\frac{C}{2}
&=90^{\circ}-\frac{A}{2}
&=180^{\circ}-(90^{\circ}+\frac{A}{2})
&=180^{\circ}-\angle BIC
\end{align*}
Hence, using the Isogonal Conjugate Lemma, there exists an isogonal conjugate  $I'$  of  $I$  in  $MNBC$ . However, since  $\angle MBI=\angle IBC$  and  $\angle NCI=\angle ICB,$   $I'$  must be  $I$  itself. Therefore,  $I$  is also the intersection of bisectors of  $\angle M$  and  $\angle N$ , along with that of  $\angle B$  and  $\angle C$ . As a result, the circle tangent to  $MB,BC,CN$ , which is the incircle of  $ABC$ , must also be tangent to  $MN.$   $\blacksquare$  ~
  $Claim.$   $Y \in (ABC)$   $Proof.$  Indeed, note that 
\begin{align*}
\angle BYC
&=360^{\circ}-\angle IBY-\angle ICY-\angle BIC
&=360^{\circ}-\angle BMI-\angle CNI-\angle BIC
&=360^{\circ}-\frac{\angle BMN}{2}-\frac{\angle CNM}{2}-\angle BIC
&=360^{\circ}-\frac{180^{\circ}-\angle AMN+180^{\circ}-\angle ANM}{2}-\angle BIC
&=360^{\circ}-\frac{180^{\circ} +\angle A}{2}-\angle BIC
&=360^{\circ}-90^{\circ}-\frac{\angle A}{2}-90^{\circ}-\frac{\angle A}{2}
&=180^{\circ}-\angle A.\blacksquare
\end{align*}
~ $Claim.$   $(MIN)$  is tangent to  $(BIC)$  $Proof.$  Indeed, note that 
\begin{align*}
\angle MIB&=180^{\circ}-\angle BMI-\angle MBI
&=180^{\circ}-\frac{\angle BMN+\angle MBC}{2}
&=\frac{360^{\circ}-\angle BMN-\angle MBC}{2}
&=\frac{\angle MNC}{2}+\frac{\angle NCB}{2}
&=\angle MNI+\angle BCI
\end{align*}
~ $Claim.$   $XI$  is the common tangent of  $(MIB)$  and  $(NIC)$  $Proof.$  Indeed, consider an inversion around  $I$ . Note that  $MNBC$  is sent to a parallelogram  $M'N'B'C'$ , since  $(MIB)$  is tangent to  $(NIC)$  and  $(BIC)$  is tangent to  $(MIN)$ . If  $P$  maps to  $P'$  and  $Q$ , to  $Q'$ , then  $P,Q,B,C$  are concyclic, along with  $\overline{M'-P'-B'}$  and  $\overline{N'-Q'-C'}$ . Now note that  $\angle P'Q'B'=180^{\circ}-\angle M'B'C'=\angle P'M'N'$ , hence  $P'M'N'Q'$  is cyclic, implying that  $PMNQ$  is also cyclic. Now, observe that by RaDax,  $BM \cap CN=X$  lies on the Radical Axis of  $(MIB)$  and  $(NIC),$  which is the common tangent.  $\blacksquare$ ~ $Claim.$   $(AMP)$  and  $(ANQ)$  are tangent. Further, the common tangent is  $XA$ .
  $Proof.$  Indeed, note that 
\begin{align*}
\angle BKJ+\angle CJK&=\angle BPJ+\angle CQK
&=\angle BPM+\angle CQN
&=\angle MIB+\angle NIC
&=180^{\circ},
\end{align*}
so  $BK \parallel CJ$ . Now note that 
\begin{align*}
\angle APM+\angle AQN&=\angle APJ+\angle AQK
&=\angle AYJ+\angle AYK
&=\angle KYJ
&=\angle A
\end{align*}
Now applying RaDaX on  $(BIM),(CIN),(AMP),$ ,  $X$  lies on radical axis of  $(AMP),(CIN)$ . Again applying RaDax on  $(AMP),(ANQ),(CIN)$ ,  $X$  lies on the radical axis of  $(AMP),(ANQ)$ , which is their common tangent.  $\blacksquare$  ~ $Claim.$   $\angle XAC=\angle YABC$  $Proof.$  Indeed, note that
