By the problem condition,  $P(z)$  and  $P\left(\frac{y+z}{2}\right)$  must lie below the line through  $P\left(\frac{x+y}{2}\right)$  parallel to  $\overline{P(x)P(y)}$ , and  $P\left(\frac{x+y}{2}\right)$  must lie below the line through  $P\left(\frac{y+z}{2}\right)$  parallel to  $\overline{P(y)P(z)}$ . However, the second condition can't be satisfied if the first condition holds, since the slopes of the red lines are greater than the slopes of the blue lines.</details>

<details><summary>Step 3</summary>Without loss of generality, assume  $z$  is closer to  $y$  than  $x$ .
