By the "concave" down condition, the function in  $x$  defined as the slope of  $\overline{P(x)P(c)}$  (ignoring the hole at  $x=c$ ) is nondecreasing. Hence  $\lim_{x \to x^-} \frac{f(c)-f(x)}{c-x}$  and  $\lim_{x \to x^+} \frac{f(c)-f(x)}{c-x}$  exist; in particular, this implies  $f$  is continuous as well.</details>

<details><summary>Step 5</summary>Suppose for contradiction that the left and right derivatives at  $x=c$  are not equal. By the concave down condition, there exist two distinct lines  $\ell_1$  and  $\ell_2$  through  $P(c)$  for which  $f(x)$  lies completely below the line, and  $f(x)$  is not tangent to either line.
