Let  $x \neq c$  be any real number and let the lines through  $P(x)$  parallel to  $\ell_1$  and  $\ell_2$  meet  $f(x)$  at  $P(z_1)$  and  $P(z_2)$ , respectively. (An intersection is guranteed to exist because  $f(x)$  is continuous and the slopes of  $\ell_1$  and  $\ell_2$  are both less than the right-derivative at  $c$ , and the intersection is unique by the concave down condition.) By the problem condition,  $c=\frac{x+z_1}{2}=\frac{x+z_2}{2}$ , so  $z_1=z_2$ , a contradiction, since the slopes of  $\ell_1$  and  $\ell_2$  are distinct.</details>

<details><summary>Step 6</summary>Since  $f$  is farthest away from the secant line through  $P(x)$  and  $P(z)$  at  $P\left(\frac{x+z}{2}\right)$  and  $f$  has a derivative, 
\[f'\left(\frac{x+z}{2}\right) = \frac{f(z)-f(x)}{z-x}.\]
In particular, this is independent of the sum of  $x$  and  $z$ .
