<details><summary>Step 7</summary>Let  $\ldots, s_{-1}, s_0, s_1, \ldots$  be an infinite arithmetic progression with common difference  $d$ . By Step 6, 
\[\frac{f(s_{n+3})-f(s_n)}{3d} = \frac{f(s_{n+2})-f(s_{n+1})}{d}\]
so 
\[(s_{i+2}-s_{i+1}) - (s_{i+1}-s_{i}) = (s_{i+3}-s_{i+2}) - (s_{i+2}-s_{i+1}).\]
Thus  $f(x)$  is quadratic over the sequence. By repeatedly halving the common difference of the arithmetic progression,  $f(x)$  must be quadratic over a dense set of  $\mathbb R$ . By continuity,  $f(x)$  must be quadratic. The concave-up quadratic solutions don't work for obvious reasons, giving the solution set in Step 1.</details>

