Define  $\Omega_A,\Omega_B$ , and  $\Omega_C$  as the circumcircles of the rectangles opposite vertex  $A,B$ , and  $C$  respectively.**Claim:**  $\Omega_A,\Omega_B$ , and  $\Omega_C$  concur.
*Proof:* Let the second intersection of  $\Omega_A$  and  $\Omega_B$  (besides  $C$ ) be  $P$ . Then, from simple angle chasing,   $$ \angle BPC=180^\circ-\angle BC_1C\text{  and  }\angle CPA=180^\circ-\angle CA_1A $$    $$ \implies \angle BPA=360^\circ-\angle BPC-\angle CPA=180^\circ-\angle AB_1B. $$  Since  $\angle AB_1B+\angle APB=180^\circ$ , points  $A,P,B$ , and  $B_1$  are concyclic, so  $\Omega_C$  passes through  $P$ , as desired.

Now, we will show that  $B_1C_2$ ,  $C_1A_2$ , and  $A_1B_2$  pass through  $P$ ; by symmetry, it suffices to show that  $B_1C_2$  passes through  $P$ . Notice that  $$ \angle B_1PB=\angle B_1AB=90^\circ-\angle AB_1B $$    $$ \angle C_2PB=\angle C_2PC+\angle CPB=\angle C_2A_1C+\angle CPB=270^\circ-\angle CA_1A-\angle BC_1C $$    $$ \implies \angle B_1PB+\angle C_2PB=360^\circ-\angle AB_1B-\angle BC_1C-\angle CA_1A=180^\circ. $$  This shows that  $B_1,P$ , and  $C_2$  are collinear, so we are finished.

