The answer is  $\boxed{a_{2k}=2,\,a_{2k+1}=1}$  for all  $k$ .

We first prove that  $a_{2k+1}\leq 1$  for all  $k$ . Suppose FTSOC  $a:=\max(a_{2k+1})>1$ , and WLOG  $a_1=a$ . Then from  $a_1=\frac{1}{a_{2n}}+\frac{1}{a_2}$  we get either  $a_2<2$  or  $a_{2n}<2$ . WLOG  $b:=a_2<2$ . We have:
\begin{align*}
a_1&=a & &
a_2&=b & &
a_3&=a_2-a_1 & &=b-a
a_4&=\frac{1}{a_3-\frac{1}{a_2}} & &=\frac{b}{b^2-ab-1}
a_5&=a_4-a_3 & &=\frac{2ab^2-b^3-a^2b+2b-a}{b^2-ab-1}
\end{align*}
Note that  $b^2-ab-1>0$  because  $a_4>0$ . Hence
\begin{align*}
& & a_1&<a_5
&\iff & a(b^2-ab-1)&<2ab^2-b^3-a^2b+2b-a
&\iff & b(b-a)&<2,
\end{align*}
which holds since  $b<2$  and  $b-a<1$ . This contradicts the maximality of  $a_1$ . Thus  $a_{2k+1}\leq 1$  for all  $k$ .

Now we have  $a_{2k}\leq 2$  for all  $k$ , so  $a_{2k+1}=\frac{1}{a_{2k}}+\frac{1}{a_{2k+2}}\geq 1$  for all  $k$ . Thus  $a_{2k+1}=1$  and  $a_{2k}=2$  for all  $k$ .  $\square$

