<details><summary>solution</summary>Note that we can pick two numbers  $x, y$  from  $\{ a, b, c\}$  that are both less than  $1$  or greater than  $1$ . This indicates that  $(x^2 - 1)(y^2 - 1) \geq 0$ , or in other words  $x^2y^2 + 1 \geq x^2 + y^2$ . Since the inequality is symmetric we can WLOG say that  $a$  and  $b$  satisfy this; in particular, multiplying by  $c^2$  gives us that  $a^2b^2c^2 \geq a^2c^2 + b^2c^2 - c^2$ .

The problem is now solved by expansion:
\begin{align*}
(a^2+2)(b^2+2)(c^2+2) &= a^2b^2c^2 + 2\sum a^2b^2 + 4 \sum a^2 + 8 
&\geq 3a^2b^2 + 3a^2c^2 + 2b^2c^2 + 3a^2 + 4b^2 + 4c^2 + 8 
&\geq 6ab + 6ac + 4bc + \frac{3a^2}{2} + \frac{3b^2}{2} + \frac{3a^2}{2} + \frac{3c^2}{2} + \frac{5b^2}{2} + \frac{5c^2}{2} 
&\geq 9(ab + ac + bc)
\end{align*}
as desired!</details>

After expanding we get our problem is equivalent to
\begin{align*}
& \qquad ~~ a^2b^2c^2 + 2 \sum a^2b^2 + 4 \sum a^2 + 8 \ge 9(ab + bc + ca) 
& \iff 2 \sum (a-b)^2 + 2 \sum (ab-1)^2 + a^2b^2c^2 + 2 - (ab + bc +ca) \ge 0
\end{align*}
So if we let  $bc - 1 = x, ca - 1 = y, ab - 1 = z$ , then it suffices to show 
\begin{align*}
2 \sum x^2 + &(x+1)(y+1)(z+1) + 2 - \bigg( (x+1)(y+1) + (y+1)(z+1) + (z+1)(x+1) \bigg) \ge 0 
&\iff 2 \sum x^2 + xyz - (xy + yx + zx) \ge 0
\end{align*}
Thus it suffices to show   $$ \sum x^2 + xyz \ge 0  $$  Note some two of  $x,y,z$  have the same sign, say  $xy \ge 0$ . Then,  $xyz \ge -xy$ , and our problem follows.  $\blacksquare$

