The answer is all pairs of the form  $(n,1)$  and  $(n,n)$  for each positive integer  $n$  only. The pairs clearly satisfy the given conditions, so we show that they are the only ones.

It is clear that if  $b=1$  then  $a$  may be any positive integer. Hence, assume  $b>1$  in what follows. 

Since  $b^2 \mid a^3$  let  $a^3 = b^2t$  for some positive integer  $t$ . Then,  $a^3-1=b^2t-1$  so  $a-1 \mid b^2t-1$ . Furthermore,  $a-1 \mid b-1$  so  $b \equiv 1 \pmod{a-1}$ . Thus,
\begin{align*}
b^2t & \equiv 1 \pmod{a-1}
t & \equiv 1 \pmod{a-1}
\end{align*}
Now, if  $t>a$ , 
\[a^3 = b^2t > ab^2 \ge a^3\]
since  $b=1$  or  $b \ge a$  due to the second condition.Thus,  $t=1$  or  $t=a$ . We split into two cases.

*Case 1 :* $t=1$ . Then,  $a^3=b^2$ . Now let  $b-1 = k(a-1)$  for some positive integer  $k$ . Then, 
\begin{align*}
a^3 &= b^2 
a^3 -1 &= b^2 -1 
a^2 + a +1 &= k(b+1) 
a^2 + a+1 &= k(k(a-1)+2)
a^2 + (1-k^2)a + (k-1)^2 &= 0 
\end{align*}
which has integral solution if and only if  $\Delta_a$  is a perfect square. Note, 
\begin{align*}
\Delta_a &= (1-k^2)^2 -4(k-1)^2 
&= (1-k^2-2k+2)(1-k^2 + 2k -2)
&= (k-1)^2((k+1)^2-4)
\end{align*}
which implies that  $(k+1)^2-4$  must be a perfect square. However, there do any exist any pair of perfect square spaced by 4 so this is impossible and this case has no valid solutions.

*Case 2 :*  $t=a$ . Now, clearly  $a=b$  and all such pairs indeed work.

