we set up the coordinate system as follows: $BC$  is the positive real axis,  $B$  is the origin  $(0,0)$  ,  $C$  is  $(c,0)$  , and  $AB$  is positive  $y$ -axis which gives  $A(0,a)$  and we also have  $D(-d,0)$  where  $a,b,c,d>0$ now we <span style="color:#f0f">**Claim:-**</span> The line  $EF$  passes through the reflection of  $C$  over  $B$ <span style="color:#073763">**Proof:-**</span> denote  $\odot_{ADC}=\omega$  and  $\odot_{DEB}=\Omega$ **<span style="color:#20124D">working for equation of  $\omega$ </span>** we have the general equation of circle as  $x^2+y^2+2gx+2fy+\ell=0$ now plugging  $(c,0) , (d,0) , (0,a)$  to this we get the equation of  $\omega$  as  $x^2+y^2+(d-c)x+y\left(\frac{dc}{a}-a\right)-dc=0$ **<span style="color:#20124D">Working for equation of  $\Omega$ :-</span>** we set the equation of the circle as  $x^2+y^2+2g'x+2f'y+\ell'=0$ now plugging  $(0,0),(-d,0)$  and  $\left(\frac{-d}{2},\frac{a}{2}\right)$  to this we get the equation of  $\Omega$  as:  $x^2+y^2+dx+y\left(\frac{d^2}{2a}-\frac{a}{2}\right)=0$ so now since we have  $\omega: x^2+y^2+(d-c)x+y\left(\frac{dc}{a}-a\right)-dc=0 \qquad$  ( $\spadesuit$ )
