**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** For every positive integer  $N$ , we can find a positive integer  $n \geq N$  with  $\gcd(n, P(n)) = 1$ .
*Proof.* Write  $P(n) = a_k n^k + \dots + a_1 n + a_0$ , and note that  $a_0 \neq 0$  since  $a_0$  is, in fact, equal to  $P(0)$ . Now
\begin{align*}
\gcd(n, P(n)) &= \gcd(n, a_k n^k + \dots + a_1 n + a_0) 
&= \gcd(n, n(a_k n^{k - 1} + \dots + a_1) + a_0) 
&= \gcd(n, a_0).
\end{align*}
Hence, we can just choose  $n$  to be a prime greater than  $\max(|a_0|, N)$ .  $\square$ Fix an index  $i \geq 2$ . Choose a large positive integer  $n$  satisfying  $\gcd(n, P(n)) = 1$ , and choose a positive integer  $M$  so that
\begin{align*}
M &\equiv 1 \pmod{n} 
M &\equiv i \pmod{n + P(n)}.
\end{align*}
(This exists by CRT since  $\gcd(n, n + P(n)) = \gcd(n, P(n)) = 1$ .) Now,
\begin{align*}
a_M &\equiv a_1 \pmod{P(n)} 
a_M &\equiv a_i \pmod{P(n + P(n))}.
\end{align*}
However,  $(n + P(n)) - n \mid P(n + P(n)) - P(n)$  since  $P$  is an integer polynomial, so in fact  $P(n) \mid P(n + P(n))$ . Therefore, \[a_1 \equiv a_i \pmod{P(n)}.\] Unless  $a_1 = a_i$ , this is absurd since we can choose  $P(n)$  to be as large as we want; but  $a_1 = a_i$  must hold for every  $i \geq 2$ , so the sequence  $a_1$ ,  $a_2$ ,  $\dots$  is constant, as desired.

