Let  $m$  and  $n$  be positive integers. We will prove that  $a_m=a_n$ . 

    By schur, take a sufficiently large prime  $r > \text{max}\{|P(0)|,|a_m-a_n|\} $  for which there exist an integer  $k$  where  $r\mid P(k)$ . Since  $r \nmid P(0)$ , we have  $r\nmid k$ . Observe that 
    \[r \ \mid P(k) \ \mid a_{i+k}-a_i \ \text{ and } \ r\ \mid P(k+r)\  \mid a_{i+k+r}-a_i \]
    for any positive integers  $i$ . Hence, for any positive integers  $i$  we have
    \begin{align*}
        a_i &\equiv a_{i+k} \pmod{r}
        a_i &\equiv a_{i+k+r} \pmod{r}
    \end{align*}
    So, the sequence  $a_1,a_2,\dots$  is periodic modulo  $r$  with period  $k$  and  $k+r$ .

    Since  $\text{gcd}(k,k+r)=\text{gcd}(k,r)= 1$ , the sequence  $a_1,a_2,\dots$  is constant modulo  $r$ . Hence,  $r\mid a_m-a_n$ , so  $a_m=a_n$  is proven.


    Since for any  $m,n$  we have  $a_m=a_n$ , the sequence  $a_1,a_2,\dots$  is constant  $\blacksquare$

