Very cute problem

----------------------------------------------------------

By pigeonhole, there exists a cycle in the states of the  $n$  cells + the rooster's location. WLOG suppose that we apply sufficiently many operations to end up in the cycle, and suppose that the  $i$ th cell counterclockwise is toggled  $2c_i$  times in the cycle.**<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** We have that  $N = \frac{3(c_1 + c_2 + \dots + c_n)}{n}$  cycles occured.
*Proof:* Each cell makes the rooster move  $3c_i$  cells.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>**   $2c_i = c_{i-1} + c_{i-2}$ .
*Proof:* Notice that when the rooster lies on the  $i$ th cell, it either came from the previous cell which was a  $0$  or the previous previous cell which was a  $1$ .  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** All  $c_i$  are equal
*Proof:* Note that if  $c_i$  is maximal for some  $i$ , then  $2c_i = c_{i-1} + c_{i-2}$  implies that  $c_{i-1}, c_{i-2}$  are likewise both maximal.  $\blacksquare$ As such, let all  $c_i$  be equal to  $c$ , so  $N = 3c$  and each cell is toggled  $2c$  times. Model the rooster as always moving at speed  $\frac{2\pi}{n}$  counterclockwise along the unit circle, toggling some cells, and let the cells be at the  $n$ th roots of unity. Say that the rooster passes over a cell  $c$  when their positions coincide.**<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** Eventually, a cell can't be toggled by the rooster at times  $k, k+n, k+2n$ .
*Proof:* We construct the pair of states  $(s_{i-1}, s_{i-2})$  of the  $i-1$ th,  $i-2$ th cell and how it evolves under the rooster passing over given that  $s_i$  is toggled.

[asy]
size(7cm);

pair p1, p2, p3, p4;
p1 = (0,0);
p2 = (1,0);
p3 = (0,1);
p4 = (1,1);

real r = 0.2;
real rs = r/sqrt(2);

filldraw(circle(p1, r), lightgrey);
filldraw(circle(p2, r), lightgrey);
filldraw(circle(p3, r), lightgrey);
filldraw(circle(p4, r), lightgrey);

label(p1, " $(1,0)$ ");
label(p2, " $(0,1)$ ");
label(p3, " $(0,0)$ ");
label(p4, " $(1,1)$ ");

draw(p4+(-rs,-rs)--p1+(rs,rs), Arrow);
draw(p2+(-rs,rs)--p3+(rs,-rs), Arrow);
draw(p2+(0,r)--p4+(0,-r), Arrow);
draw(p3+(r,0)--p4+(-r,0), Arrow);
draw(p3+(0,-r)--p1+(0,r), Arrow);

[/asy]

Notably, the only chain of length  $3$  that can occur is  $(0,1) \to (1,1) \to (1,0)$ , but thus this only happens if a cell is toggled at times  $k-2, k+n-2, k+2n-2$  if  $k \ge 2$ . If  $n$  is odd, this implies that all sufficiently large times  $t$  are toggles, which can't occur. If  $n$  is even, this implies that all sufficiently large times of the same parity  $2k+\varepsilon$ , so thus cells of some parity, are toggles, which also can't occur.  $\blacksquare$ Now, for any cell, let  $t_0, t_1, t_2, \dots$  be whether a toggle occured at the  $i$ th it is passed, which is periodic with period  $N = 3c$ . Then we take a run  $t_{i+1}, t_{i+2}, \dots, t_{i+N}$ , we have there are  $2c$  toggles among these  $3c$  elements, and at most  $2$  toggles per run of  $3$  elements. This can only happen if there are  $2$  toggles in each run of length  $3$ . Taking  $i$  as different values  $\pmod{3}$  gives that all sufficiently large runs  $t_{i+1}, t_{i+2}, t_{i+3}$  have exactly  $2$  toggles. 

This condition then uniquely determines the sequence  $t_i$  as a shift of  $011011011 \dots$ , which means that  $t_i$  is periodic with length  $3$ , so we can in fact take  $N = 3$ ,  $c = 1$  to finish (as the rooster can be checked to make exactly  $3$  cycles).

